    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q8-question-1.svg\" alt=\"It is given that a and b are positive odd integers, with  a &gt; b . Use proof by contradiction to show that if  a + b is a multiple of 4 , then a − b cannot be a multiple of 4 . ( ) 5\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q8-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Suppose both a + b and a −b were multiples of 4. [B1] [A1] Then a + b = 4m, a −b = 4n for integers m, n. Adding gives 2a = 4(m + n), hence a = 2(m + n). Their sum 2a would then be a multiple of 4, forcing a even: [M1] [A1] This contradicts the hypothesis that a is odd . [B1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
