    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q10-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The figure below shows a square ABCD of side length 2a cm, circumscribed by a circle. Four semicircles are then drawn outside the square having each of the sides of the square as a diameter. A B 2a 2a lunes C D\" style=\"--madas-width:546.7px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q10-question-2.svg\" alt=\"Each of the four regions bounded by a semicircle and the circumscribing circle is known by the mathematical name of a “lune”, i.e. moon shaped. ( ) Show that the area of the four lunes is equal to the area of the square ABCD . 8\" style=\"--madas-width:578.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"√ The radius of the circumscribed circle is half the square’s diagonal, namely a 2. [B1] [A1] The diagonal is √ q (2a)2 + (2a)2 = 8a2 . √ = 2a 2 The circle area is 2πa2, which is also the sum of the areas of the four semicircles of radius a. [M1] [A1] Inside the circle, the four semicircles cover the four circle segments outside the square: [A1] One circle segment has area [2πa2 −4a2]/4 = (π −2)a2/2.\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-u/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Subtract this from the semicircle to find one lune: πa2/2 −(π −2)a2/2 = a2. The four therefore total 4a2. Subtract these segments from both equal areas: [A1] The circle leaves the square and the semicircles leave the four lunes, so both have area 4a2 cm2 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 10 is 8 marks.</p>"
